{"records":[{"id":"2v5jg2zksj","title":"TMEM79/MATTRIN defines a pathway for Frizzled regulation and is required for Xenopus embryogenesis","description":"Absctract\nWnt signaling through the Frizzled (FZD) family of serpentine receptors is essential for embryogenesis and homeostasis, and stringent control of the FZD protein level is critical for stem cell regulation. Through CRISPR/Cas9 genome-wide screening in human cells, we identified TMEM79/MATTRIN, an orphan multi-span transmembrane protein, as a specific inhibitor of Wnt/FZD signaling. TMEM79 interacts with FZD during biogenesis and promotes FZD degradation independent of ZNRF3/RNF43 ubiquitin ligases (R-spondin receptors). TMEM79 interacts with ubiquitin-specific protease 8 (USP8), whose activating mutations underlie human tumorigenesis. TMEM79 specifically inhibits USP8 deubiquitination of FZD, thereby governing USP8 substrate specificity and promoting FZD degradation. Tmem79 and Usp8 genes have a pre-bilaterian origin, and Tmem79 inhibition of Usp8 and Wnt signaling is required for anterior neural development and gastrulation in Xenopus embryos. TMEM79 is a predisposition gene for Atopic dermatitis, suggesting deregulation of Wnt/FZD signaling a possible cause for this most common yet enigmatic inflammatory skin disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-09-26T05:47:58.606Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2v5jg2zksj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2v5jg2zksj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maorong","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nathalia","last_name":"Amado","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jieqiong","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alice ","last_name":"Reis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mengxu","last_name":"Ge","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose","last_name":"Abreu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xi","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hkr6z3rvmh","title":"A Tunable Brake for HECT Ubiquitin Ligases","description":"This data set includes the coomassie staining gel images, western blot images, and fluorescence imaging results for the paper. The original data for the figures in the paper and the corresponding replicates are uploaded.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-05-11T18:44:47.275Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hkr6z3rvmh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hkr6z3rvmh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zan","last_name":"chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Fluorescence Imaging","Gel"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"bkwjctz23n","title":"Regulation of phosphoribosyl-linked serine ubiquitination by deubiquitinases DupA and DupB, Shin et al","description":"The family of bacterial SidE enzymes catalyzes non-canonical phosphoribosyl-linked (PR) serine ubiquitination and promotes infectivity of Legionella pneumophila, a pathogenic bacterium causing Legionnaires’ disease. Here we describe identification of two bacterial effectors (LaiE and LaiF) that actively reverse PR-ubiquitination during infection and are thus named DeUbiquitinases for PR-ubiquitination (DUPs; DupA/LaiE and DupB/LaiF). Structural analyses revealed that DupA and SidE ubiquitin ligases harbor a highly homologous catalytic phosphodiesterase (PDE) domain. However, unlike SidE ubiquitin ligases, DupA displays increased affinity to PR-ubiquitinated substrates, which dictates its catalytic activity allowing DupA to predominantly cleave PR-ubiquitin from substrates. Interfering with DupA-ubiquitin binding switches its activity toward SidE-type ligase. Given the high affinity of DupA to PR-ubiquitinated substrates, we next exploited a catalytically inactive DupA mutant to trap and subsequently identify more than 180 PR-ubiquitinated host proteins in Legionella-infected cells. Proteins involved in endoplasmic reticulum (ER) fragmentation and membrane recruitment to Legionella-containing vacuoles (LCV) emerged as major SidE targets. The global map of PR-ubiquitinated substrates uncovered by DupA-mediated trapping strategy provides critical insights into host-pathogen interactions during Legionella infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-31T15:44:44.544Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bkwjctz23n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bkwjctz23n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Dikic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Donghyuk","last_name":"Shin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rukmini","last_name":"Mukherjee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yaobin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexis","last_name":"Gonzalez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Structural Biology","Endoplasmic Reticulum","Ubiquitylation","Legionella"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cdgx63gd5y","title":"TRIM21 induces selective autophagy of viruses and bacteria","description":"TRIM21 is an exceptionally versatile ubiquitin ligase that can be directed by antibodies to target oligomeric protein scaffolds, viral capsids, and proteopathic aggregates for intracellular degradation. How the cell degrades these typically resistant substrates remains poorly understood. To address this, we used TRIM21 viral restriction to create a genome-wide phenotypic screen for antibody-dependent capsid degradation. We identify an antimicrobial selective macroautophagy pathway in mammalian cells, which we term \"antibody-directed xenophagy.\" We show this mechanism restricts structurally diverse pathogens, including adenovirus and Salmonella. Using quantitative microscopy, we demonstrate that TRIM21 rapidly intercepts antibody-pathogen complexes, leading to ubiquitin ligase activation. Following this, selective autophagy adaptors are recruited and viral cargoes are delivered to lysosomes. This process reduces Salmonella pathology and bacterial tissue invasion in mice. We propose that TRIM21 evolved through competition with pathogens to induce autophagy of diverse and complex substrates, potentially explaining its versatility for targeted protein degradation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-29T20:32:28.807Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cdgx63gd5y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cdgx63gd5y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tyler","last_name":"Rhinesmith","orcid":"0000-0002-9983-0287"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03x94j517","name":"Medical Research Council","grant_id":"U105181010"},{"id":"https://ror.org/029chgv08","name":"Wellcome Trust","grant_id":"223054/Z/21/Z"}],"categories":["Protein Degradation","Autophagy","Host Immune Response","Antiviral Immunity","Bacterial Host Response","Ubiquitin Ligase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vv8spnwgyr","title":"Activity-based proteomic dataset for EGF stimulated HEK293T cells","description":"These raw data are from proteomic experiments where HEK293T cells have been treated with or without epidermal growth factor. Using a photocrosslinking activity-based probe for ubiquitin E3 ligases, the treated and untreated cells are profiled. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-04T12:47:05.071Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vv8spnwgyr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vv8spnwgyr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Satpal","last_name":"Virdee","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Structural Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jpcwgwszz4","title":"Source Data: Molecular mechanisms of CAND2 in regulating SCF ubiquitin ligases","description":"Source data for the published research article. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-06-19T14:38:38.905Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jpcwgwszz4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jpcwgwszz4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xing","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/013kjyp64","name":"American Heart Association","grant_id":"20CDA35270030"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R35GM138016"}],"categories":["Biochemistry","Protein","Degradation Mechanism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"32h94v2yv2","title":"Elucidation of E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Specificity . Zhang et al.Through Proteome-wide Degron Mapping. Zhang et al.","description":"This repository contains sequencing data related to \"Elucidation of E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Specificity Through Proteome-wide Degron Mapping\" by Zhang et al. Data is included for the GPS peptidome screen with and without MLN, results from the composition-based SVM machine learning model, along with sequencing data for scanning and saturation mutagenesis degron footprints, and from CRISPR screens for identification of E3-degron pairs. Westernblot raw images from cycloheximide chase and co-immunoprecipitation assays are also included.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-21T06:47:33.015Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/32h94v2yv2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/32h94v2yv2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhiqian","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brandon","last_name":"Sie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aiquan","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yumei","last_name":"Leng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christopher","last_name":"Nardone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stephen ","last_name":"Elledge","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein Degradation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5v545cw8t7","title":"Epstein-Barr virus BNRF1 destabilizes SMC5/6 cohesin complexes to evade its restriction of replication compartments. Yiu et al.","description":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) persistently infects most people worldwide. Delivery of ~170 kilobase EBV genomes to nuclei, and use of nuclear membrane-less replication compartments (RC) for their lytic cycle amplification, necessitate evasion of intrinsic antiviral responses. Proteomic analysis identified that upon B-cell infection or lytic reactivation, EBV depletes the chromosome maintenance cohesin SMC5/6, which has major chromosome maintenance roles and DNA damage repair. The major tegument protein BNRF1 targeted SMC5/6 complexes by a ubiquitin proteasome pathway dependent on calpain proteolysis and cullin-7. In the absence of BNRF1, SMC5/6 associated with R-loop structures, including at the viral lytic origin of replication, and interfered with RC formation and encapsidation. CRISPR analysis highlighted RC restriction roles of SMC5/6 components involved in DNA entrapment and SUMOylation. Our studies highlight SMC5/6 as a key intrinsic immune sensor and restriction factor for a human herpesvirus RC and have implications for the pathogenesis of EBV-associated cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-03T06:55:14.952Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5v545cw8t7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5v545cw8t7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Stephanie Pei Tung","last_name":"Yiu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9s37k6s8jr","title":"Crucial Roles of the BRCA1-BARD1 E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Activity in Homology-Directed DNA Repair","description":"A pdf file containing raw images, gel images and western blots for manuscript entitled 'Crucial Roles of the BRCA1-BARD1 E3 Ubiquitin Ligase Activity in Homology-Directed DNA Repair' by Wang & Li et al, 2023.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-04T06:46:46.997Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9s37k6s8jr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9s37k6s8jr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weixing","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenjing","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","DNA Repair","DNA Damage Response"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":142507,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":170,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":142507,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":141746,"restricted":70,"embargoed":691},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://r